Configuration parser test (parser/)
===================================

The 'parser' test checks the OTel filter configuration parser against the rules
of README-implementation section 4.5 "Keyword Definition Rules".  Every case is
one 'haproxy -c' run over a generated OTel configuration file: how often each
keyword may be written, what makes a repetition an error, and where a trailing
'if'/'unless' condition is permitted.

The cases reach past those rules as well: to what a keyword accepts as its own
arguments (their number, the characters of the name it defines, the values it
names and the sample expressions written after its key), to the freedom to write
the sections of one OTel scope in any order, and to the validation that runs
once the whole HAProxy configuration is known.

The test never starts a proxy and never opens a socket.  The runtime side of
the rules cannot be seen at configuration load time and is left to the tests
that run traffic: the first-match evaluation of the lines of one key, and the
rule that a span or an instrument name defined in more than one otel-scope is
created by exactly one of those scopes.


Files
-----

  run-parser.sh          test driver, run it from the test directory
  parser/rules.tab       machine-readable transcription of section 4.5
  parser/gen.awk         expands rules.tab into case records
  parser/build.awk       turns case records into configuration files
  parser/cover.awk       reports the keywords of include/parser.h with no case
  parser/rules.awk       reports the keyword entries of section 4.5 with no case
  parser/cases/*.cases   the cases that rules.tab cannot express
  parser/haproxy.cfg     HTTP-mode proxy holding the filter declaration
  parser/haproxy-*.cfg   the proxy variants a case selects with its '@mode'
  parser/otel.yml        minimal SDK pipeline, needed for the YAML validation
  parser/otel-invalid.yml a pipeline the SDK refuses, for the rejection case
  _logs/_log-run-parser-* the alert and warning text of every case of one run


Running
-------

  % ./run-parser.sh [-k <pattern>] [-x] [-X] [-v] [-h] [<haproxy>]

    -k <pattern>  run only the cases whose name matches the pattern
    -x            keep the configuration file of every failed case in _logs
    -X            keep the configuration file of every case, failed or not
    -v            show the alert text of every case, not only of the failed
    -h            show the help and exit

The HAProxy executable defaults to ../../haproxy/haproxy.  The exit status is 0
when every case holds, EX_SOFTWARE (70) when one fails, and the full alert and
warning text of the run is kept in the log file named on the last output line.

The generated configuration files live in a temporary directory that is removed
when the driver exits; '-x' copies the file of a failed case into _logs so
that it can be checked by hand, and '-X' the file of every case.  The path of
the file of the case being checked is passed to HAProxy in the OTEL_PARSER_CFG
environment variable, which the proxy configurations expand on their 'filter
opentelemetry' line, the one that names no file aside.

Every case runs against the proxy configuration its '@mode' field names: 'http'
takes parser/haproxy.cfg and any other name parser/haproxy-<name>.cfg.  A new
proxy variant is one file and no change to the driver, and a mode whose file
is missing fails the case instead of falling back to another proxy.


How a case is judged
--------------------

A case declares whether 'haproxy -c' must accept or reject the configuration
and, when it must reject it, the alert text the rejection has to carry.  The
text matters: without it a case passes as soon as the configuration is refused
for any reason at all, and a rule that no longer holds would still look tested
because some unrelated error, a misspelled sample or an invalid character in a
key, hides the missing check.  build.awk therefore refuses an error case that
names no text.

A case that must be accepted fails when an alert is emitted.  It may also name
a warning that the run has to print, or one that it must not print: the missing
root mark is a warning, and the second direction is what shows that the spans
of a scope which never runs do not count towards it.  The other warnings are
only recorded in the log, some of them depending on how the filter was built.

A case may also name an alert that the run must not print.  The cases reading
one file through two filter lines need it: the second parse must not repeat
or invent an error about the first one, as it did while an aborted parse left
its section state behind.  A run that ends in a crash fails whatever the case
expects.

The text of a case that checks which line an alert names carries the line number
as well: 'idle-timeout' and 'inject use-headers' pair with the scope event, so
the alert has to fall on the line that completes the pair, whichever of the two
comes second.


The rules table
---------------

parser/rules.tab holds one row per keyword and the list of shapes to generate
for it.  A shape is a repetition or condition pattern whose verdict follows
from section 4.5: the bare line of a key is the default and must be the last
one, so 'barecond' is an error while 'condbare' is not.  The row may override
the verdict of a single shape, and that is how the documented irregularities
are declared: 'log-record' repeats freely, a repeated bare 'span' line only
re-activates the span, and 'otel-event' is allowed at most once.  The 'acl' rows
instead list the forbidden-condition shapes with no alert text, which declares
that a condition after an 'acl' pattern is not caught at all.  The 'instrument'
row writes the create form, so the additive update form has cases of its own.
Where an entry names a scheme per form, as 'instrument' and 'event' do, the
row covers one of them and a case tagged 'applyall' stands for the apply-all
one.

The two condition slots differ in spelling, the first written with 'if' and the
second with 'unless', so a shape that writes two conditions exercises both of
them; the 'unlessforbid' shape is the 'unless' counterpart of 'condforbid'.

The 'fewargs' and 'badname' shapes stand outside section 4.5 and follow the
keyword tables of include/parser.h instead: 'fewargs' writes the keyword alone,
without any of the arguments that it requires, and 'badname' gives its key a
character that the name class does not allow.  Each carries its own alert text.

The format of the table and the list of shapes are described in the header of
the file itself.  Adding a keyword there is one row; a rule that changes in
section 4.5 is one field.


The case files
--------------

parser/cases/*.cases hold the cases that no shape can express: the top-level
OTel scope rules, the scope that a name on the filter line picks out, a name
repeated within one line, a section header written without its name or falling
in another OTel scope, the pairings checked once a section has been parsed, and
the file-level oddities such as CRLF line endings or a missing final newline.  A
few of them pin the reach of the schemes: one key written bare on two spans of
one otel-scope, or on one span of two otel-scopes, is a later operation and not
a repetition, so it is accepted, and a scope-level keyword compares its lines
within one otel-scope only, while a span re-activated in another otel-scope
carries a context of its own there.  The record format itself is described in
the header of parser/build.awk.

The args.cases and check.cases files carry what reaches past the definition
rules.  In the first a keyword is written with a value it cannot take, with a
badly formed argument, with more arguments than it accepts or with a sample
expression that does not resolve, and the alert then has to name the faulty
argument.  In the second the file parses cleanly and the error is only found
afterwards, once the proxy and the rest of the configuration are known: the
'require-context' rules, the header operations a TCP-mode proxy cannot carry,
an instrumentation that names no configuration file, a name that a 'parent', a
'link', a 'finish' or a 'set-var-ctx' refers to and that no otel-scope defines,
a filter ID that another instance already carries, the names an 'otel-group'
action resolves, and the warnings that the whole configuration decides.


Coverage
--------

After the run the driver reads the keyword tables of include/parser.h and then
reports every keyword that no case covers, which makes the test fail.  A keyword
added to the parser therefore cannot land untested.

A second report reads section 4.5 itself and holds the case set against it: a
keyword entry of the section must have a case, an entry that names a repetition
scheme must have a rules.tab row whose shapes carry that scheme, and a forbidden
condition must be tested exactly when the entry allows none.  A rule rewritten
in the section without the matching case therefore fails the test as well, which
the keyword report alone cannot see.

A keyword that has no rules.tab row carries the forbidden condition on a case
of its own, which names the shape it stands in for in its '@rule' field.  The
field takes the shape names of rules.tab, so a hand-written case counts for
the report the same way a generated one does, and it takes 'applyall' as well,
which no shape writes: it marks the case standing for the apply-all form of
an entry whose row writes the other one.

The same report reads the paragraphs that stand before the keyword entries for
one rule of their own: every clause they name as writable once needs a case
that writes it twice on one line.  Such a case says so with a 'clause:<name>'
tag in its '@rule' field, so a clause named in the section without the matching
case fails the run.

Both reports are skipped when the case set is filtered with '-k'.
